republicans have just won a major  victory at the supreme court over a  controversial new congressional map in  the state of alabama now the voting  rights advocates say the  republican-drawn map dilutes the power  of black voters and violates federal law  but that is not how a majority of the  justices saw it jan crawford is at the  supreme court with more on the story jan  good morning the justices have spoken  they have for now gail i mean the court  is siding with state republicans and  said it was just too close to alabama's  election absentee voting is starting in  just seven weeks to completely redraw  the state's legislative district so that  means the republican drawn map will stay  in place this year with just one black  majority district in a state where black  people make up 27 percent of the  population the five conservative  justices voted to block a lower court  ruling that had ordered the state to  draw a new map just as brett kavanaugh  explained it's one thing for a state on  its own to toy with election laws but  it's quite another thing for a federal  court to swoop in and redo a state  selection laws in the period close to an  election dissenting justice selena kagan  wrote alabama is not entitled to keep  violating black alabamians voting rights  just because the court's order came down  in the first month of an election year  chief justice john roberts and the three  liberal justices would have allowed a  new court-ordered map with two black  majority districts to stand while the  legal challenge continues and with the  court already agreeing to take on an  affirmative action case next year in  university admissions this means two  racially charged issues are certain to  be at the forefront of those  confirmation hearings for the new  justice which president biden has said  will be a black woman  tony  jan thank you very much
 

